# Lecture 09B

## Crossover Interference

## Chapter 8

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 3, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Identify reciprocal double-crossover types in a test cross.
- Calculate expected doubles, coincidence, and interference.
- Explain why outer-pair recombination differs from map length.

=== layout: one-panel

# Why Do the Distances Not Add?

@body

![Recombination Frequencies](assets/lect09b-crossover_interference/b_cn_vg.svg)

Wait: *9% + 9.5% = 18.5%*, but the outside pair gives *17%*.
What is missing from the outside-pair comparison?

=== layout: one-panel

# Double Crossovers Can Be Hidden

@body

| Chromosome | A | B | C |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Parental | + | + | + |
| After one crossover in each interval | + | b | + |

- The middle allele changes, but the outside combination is still + / +.
- Each double contributes once to each adjacent interval.
- The outside-pair comparison can miss both exchanges.

This is why a map can be longer than its observed outer-pair recombination suggests.

=== layout: definitions

# Genetic Interference

@body

- Positive interference: a crossover reduces the chance of another crossover nearby.
- Independent crossovers: an event in one interval does not change the probability in the other.
- Coefficient of coincidence: observed doubles divided by expected doubles.
- Interference: one minus the coefficient of coincidence.

=== include: lect09b1-interference_worked_example.djot

=== layout: one-panel

# Mapping Functions

@body

![Recombination frequency and map distance under different interference assumptions](assets/lect09b-crossover_interference/image_004.jpg)

@citation

Recombination approaches 50%; map distance can grow. Mapping functions assume an interference model.

=== layout: two-panels

# Chromatin Packaging

@left

- *Euchromatin*: loosely packed; often more transcriptionally active.
- *Heterochromatin*: densely packed; often less transcriptionally active.

These are differences in packaging within the nucleus.

@right

![Loosely packed euchromatin and densely packed heterochromatin](assets/lect09b-crossover_interference/image_007.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Genetic and Physical Distance

@body

![Physical and genetic maps compare euchromatin with heterochromatin](assets/lect09b-crossover_interference/image_006.jpg)

@citation

Here: 25% physical length; 3/107 = 2.8% map length. A cM is not a fixed DNA length.

=== layout: one-panel

# Fewer Crossovers Is a Different Claim

@body

| Measurement | What Does It Ask? |
| --- | --- |
| Recombination frequency | How often are the markers recombined? |
| Interference | How do observed doubles compare with independent expectations? |

- Recombination is suppressed in many heterochromatic regions.
- Fewer crossovers alone does not demonstrate stronger interference.
- A linkage map reports recombination, not gene-expression activity.

=== layout: section

# Reference Problems

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: Lz-Su-Gl

@body

| lz | su | gl | Count |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Lz | Su | Gl | 286 |
| lz | su | gl | 272 |
| Lz | su | gl | 40 |
| lz | Su | Gl | 33 |
| Lz | Su | gl | 59 |
| lz | su | Gl | 44 |
| Lz | su | Gl | 4 |
| lz | Su | gl | 2 |


There are *740 offspring*. The gene order is *lz-su-gl*.
Capitals mark the wild-type allele in this table. Calculate the interference.

=== layout: one-panel

# The Outer Pair Misses Six Doubles

@body

| Event Type | Count |
| --- | --- |
| SCO in lz-su | 40 + 33 = 73 |
| SCO in su-gl | 59 + 44 = 103 |
| DCO | 4 + 2 = 6 |

- Outside-pair recombinants: 73 + 103 = *176*; 176 / 740 = *23.8%*.
- Restore both DCO contributions: 176 + 2(6) = *188*.
- Interval sum: 188 / 740 x 100 = *25.4 cM*, about 10.7 + 14.7 cM.

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: Expected Doubles

@body

| Adjacent Interval | Count-Based Frequency |
| --- | --- |
| lz-su | (40 + 33 + 4 + 2) / 740 = 79 / 740 |
| su-gl | (59 + 44 + 4 + 2) / 740 = 109 / 740 |

$$
E_{\mathrm{DCO}} = \frac{79}{740} \times \frac{109}{740} \times 740 \approx 11.6365
$$

The map labels round to 10.7 and 14.7 cM. Keep the count-based fractions until the final answer.

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: Lz-Su-Gl Answer

@body

| Quantity | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Observed doubles | 4 + 2 = 6 |
| Expected doubles | 8611 / 740 (about 11.6365) |
| Coincidence | 4440 / 8611 (about 0.5156) |
| Interference | 1 - CoC: about 0.4844, or 48.4% |

There are fewer doubles than expected: *positive interference*.
Use unrounded values during the calculation.

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: A-C-B Problem

@body

| A | B | C | Count | Phenotype |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| + | + | + | 158 | wildtype |
| + | + | c | 209 | conehead |
| + | b | + | 30 | bald |
| + | b | c | 1106 | bald, conehead |
| a | + | + | 1138 | amber |
| a | + | c | 24 | amber, conehead |
| a | b | + | 187 | amber, bald |
| a | b | c | 148 | amber, bald, conehead |


Order: *A-C-B*. Adjacent intervals: *12 cM* and *15 cM*. Total: *3,000 offspring*.
What is the interference?

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: A-C-B Answer

@body

| Quantity | Calculation |
| --- | --- |
| Observed doubles | +b+ and a+c: 30 + 24 = 54 |
| Expected doubles | 0.12 x 0.15 x 3,000 = 54 |
| Coincidence | 54 / 54 = 1 |
| Interference | 1 - 1 = 0, or 0% |

Observed = expected: *no interference* in this dataset.
The table gives 23.4% outer-pair recombination; the adjacent intervals add to 27 cM.

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: F-G-H Problem

@body

| F | G | H | Count |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| F | G | H | 4 |
| F | G | h | 41 |
| F | g | H | 393 |
| F | g | h | 50 |
| f | G | H | 64 |
| f | G | h | 413 |
| f | g | H | 33 |
| f | g | h | 2 |


There are *1,000 offspring*. Find the parents, doubles, and interval frequencies.
Then calculate interference. Capitals mark wild-type alleles in this example.

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: F-G-H Mapping

@body

| Item | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Parents | FgH / fGh |
| Double-crossover types | FGH and fgh; 4 + 2 = 6 |
| Gene order | F-G-H or H-G-F |
| F-G recombinants | 41 + 33 + 6 = 80; 8 cM |
| G-H recombinants | 50 + 64 + 6 = 120; 12 cM |

What interference do these counts imply?

=== layout: one-panel

# Reference: F-G-H Answer

@body

| Quantity | Calculation |
| --- | --- |
| Independent DCO fraction | 0.08 x 0.12 = 0.0096 |
| Expected doubles | 0.0096 x 1,000 = 9.6 |
| Coincidence | 6 / 9.6 = 0.625, or 62.5% |
| Interference | 1 - 0.625 = 0.375, or 37.5% |

We observed *37.5% fewer doubles* than independence predicts.

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Use adjacent interval probabilities to predict independent doubles.
- Multiply by all offspring to turn the expected fraction into a count.
- Coincidence is observed / expected; interference is one minus coincidence.
- Positive interference means fewer doubles than independence predicts.

